          { difficulty: 'medium', question: "A sample of 100 students has mean GPA = 3.2, SD = 0.4. Calculate the 95% confidence interval for the population mean.", questionRu: "Выборка 100 учеников: среднее GPA = 3,2, SD = 0,4. Найдите 95% доверительный интервал.", questionKk: "100 оқушы іріктемесі: орташа GPA = 3,2, SD = 0,4. 95% сенімділік аралығын есептеңіз.", answer: "95% CI = 3.2 ± 1.96 × (0.4/√100) = 3.2 ± 1.96 × 0.04 = 3.2 ± 0.078 = (3.122, 3.278). We are 95% confident the true population mean GPA is between 3.12 and 3.28.", hint: "Use: x̄ ± 1.96 × (SD/√n).", hintRu: "Формула: x̄ ± 1,96 × (SD/√n).", hintKk: "Формула: x̄ ± 1,96 × (SD/√n).", xp: 10 },
          { difficulty: 'hard', question: "Two studies test the same hypothesis. Study 1 (n=30): mean difference = 10 points, p = 0.04. Study 2 (n=500): mean difference = 2 points, p = 0.001. Which study found a more practically important result? Explain the difference between statistical and practical significance.", questionRu: "Два исследования: Study 1 (n=30): разница 10 баллов, p=0,04. Study 2 (n=500): разница 2 балла, p=0,001. Какое практически важнее? Объясните разницу.", questionKk: "Екі зерттеу: 1 (n=30): 10 балл айырмашылық, p=0,04. 2 (n=500): 2 балл, p=0,001. Қайсысы практикалық тұрғыдан маңыздырақ? Айырмашылықты түсіндіріңіз.", answer: "Study 1 is more practically important — a 10-point difference is meaningful in most contexts. Study 2 has stronger statistical significance (smaller p) but only a 2-point difference, which may be trivial. Large samples can make tiny differences statistically significant. Statistical significance tells you the result isn't due to chance; practical significance tells you the result matters in the real world.", xp: 20 }
